ifts and also the  weather features that  help with the spawn rates and so on for  pokemon  i'm currently the lead sre on the harry  potter wizards unite title  and support several back-end services  and monitoring that are used by current  and future titles  our games are enjoyed by millions of  players worldwide  i'm going to be a little bit vague about  scale but i will say that we do  have a large number of players we have  players that want to play the game 24x7  they want the game to work  whenever and wherever they decide to  play we run on the google cloud platform  and make heavy use of the kubernetes  engine spanner data flow cloud load  balancer and several other gcp services  our architecture is somewhere between  monolithic and a microservice model  we have games that consist of several  discrete components  including the front-end server and  several back-end services to support  the games so the front-end servers take  care of things like keeping track of the  game state player activity keeping track  of game rules  the back-end services take care of  things such as supporting our spatial  database  uh player account registry and then some  of the social features  again the gifting and friends list  all right thank you um so one of the  things you just mentioned were services  so  tell me about how do you monitor the  health of these services  when i started we were using a  traditional monitoring approach so  a combination of prometheus and google  cloud monitoring we actually like the  way that the two work together quite  nicely  and so we support both the  traditional monitoring approach means  that you pick a set of metrics that you  think represent  how well the system is working so that's  this will be a mix of  system metrics like memory and cpu use  and then  game level metrics that might be things  like how many pokemon are being captured  how many spells are being cast how many  players are logged in  you then set thresholds for normal and 