lk about you know what do  you do when the metrics that you want  isn't there how can you go about  adding custom metrics to google cloud  monitoring  in order to use them as service level  indicators  i'm going to discuss you know four major  options right so again these are metrics  that aren't there for you out of the box  so using load balancer metrics that you  might have already talked about would be  a great place to start  that's an option for you i'm going to  talk about specifically  cases where the metrics just don't exist  what can you do so i'm going to discuss  four options  log base metrics uh using the monitoring  api directly  using a custom instrumentation library  like open census or open telemetry  or using prometheus which is a  well-known open source time series  monitoring toolkit  and as i go down this list i'm going to  talk about kind of the basics of each  one  how you might go about implementing it  and then talk about some pros and cons  of each so you can kind of understand  what are the trade-offs of all these  different approaches  so let's start by talking about log base  metrics right fundamentally log base  metrics are the idea that you can take  numerical values in your logs payloads  and convert them into metrics out of the  box we ship  uh metrics that are called system  logging metrics right uh so these are  things that measure for example  log volume ingestions and other things  that you can understand you know how  much logging is happening within your  project  uh you know and really how much that's  going to cost you  you can then define two different uh  metric  measure types based on your application  or system logs  counter metrics would be just things  that kind of tell you how many times  do you see entries that match the  particular filter  right distribution metrics are primarily  what you would use for slis because  you can actually use them to extract um  using a regular expression numerical  values from the logs p